    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13267",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\geq -1.16).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0869 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1075 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1056 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1038 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1271 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1251 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1230 \\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z \\geq -1.16).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/cac32ab36217820b63574e011488721bf57652fa63b4cbb7e5029fb54ce26243.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.16) & =1-P(Z<-1.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/48adcb6bd3b0d12b9bc935b2414d615e0014d905b89a9f7d632306cd30f08580.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-1.16) =  0.1230.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.16) & =1-Φ(-1.16) \\\\  & =1-0.1230 \\\\  & =0.8770.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
